{
  "$schema": "https://aka.ms/codetour-schema",
  "title": "#4 - Create Okta Application Integrations",
  "steps": [
    {
      "title": "Lab overview",
      "description": "## Create Okta Application Integrations\n 🎯 **Objective**    Create Okta application integrations for two existing applications. Assign one application to the Customers group and assign one application to the Franchisee group.\n \n  🎬 **Scenario**     Customers and Franchisees require access to a different set of applications.\n\n  ⏱️ **Duration**    15 minutes\n"
    },
    {
      "description": "## Enable Cross-Origin Resource Sharing (CORS)\n\nIn Okta, **CORS** allows JavaScript hosted on your website to make a request using an\n`XMLHttpRequest` to the Okta API. While we aren't hosting the Okta Sign-In Widget when we use the Redirect Model, our sample applications ***do*** call out to Okta's API to close an Okta session and log a user out. Every website origin must be explicitly permitted as a **Trusted Origin** in your Okta org. \n\n1. Ensure you are logged in to the Admin Dashboard as Super Admin.\n\n2. In the Admin menu, navigate to **Security** > **API**\n\n3. Select on the **Trusted Origins**  tab.\n\n4. Select the **Add Origin** button\n\n5. Enter **Alglens Scoop Shop's Portal** into the **Origin name** field.\n\n6. In the **Origin URL** field, enter the value that prints out to the terminal when you select [here](command:codetour.sendTextToTerminal?[\"echo https://$CODESPACE_NAME-8080.app.github.dev\"]).\n\n\n7. Under **Choose Type**, select **Cross-Origin Resource Sharing (CORS)** \n\n6. Select **Save**"
    },
    {
      "title": "Create an Application Integration for the Rewards app",
      "description": "## Create an Application Integration for the Rewards app\nThe Rewards app is an existing application in this project. We're going to set up an integration in Okta so our customers can access this application. We'll talk about the code implementation in subsequent modules.\n\n1. In the Admin menu, navigate to **Applications** > **Applications**\n\n1. Select **Create App Integration**\n\n2. Select the **OIDC - OpenID Connect** radio button.\n\n3. Select the **Single-Page Application** radio button.\n\n4. Select **Next**\n\n5. Enter **Customer Rewards** as the Application integration name\n\n6. In the **Sign-in redirect URIs** field, enter the value that prints out to the terminal when you select [here](command:codetour.sendTextToTerminal?[\"echo https://$CODESPACE_NAME-8080.app.github.dev/redirect/rewards\"]).\n\n7. In the **Sign-out redirect URIs** field, enter the value that prints out to the terminal when you select [here](command:codetour.sendTextToTerminal?[\"echo https://$CODESPACE_NAME-8080.app.github.dev\"]).\n\n7. Under **Assignments** select the radio button option for **Limit access to select groups**\n\n8. Type in and select **Customers**\n\n9. Select **Save**",
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 16,
          "character": 152
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 16,
          "character": 222
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "file": "redirect/rewards",
      "description": "## Configure the Rewards application `appClientId`\n\nThe Rewards application makes use of Okta's AuthJS SDK, which we will learn about more in the next module. For now, you'll simply need to configure the ClientID and Okta Org URL to make this integration work. We're going to use [account-specific GitHub secrets](https://docs.github.com/en/codespaces/managing-your-codespaces/managing-your-account-specific-secrets-for-github-codespaces#adding-a-secret) for this.\n\nFirst, we'll create `REWARDS_CLIENT_ID`, which will be read in on the line highlighted above.\n\n1. Copy the `Client ID` that was displayed after you saved your integration. \n\n2. In a new browser tab, go to https://github.com\n\n3. On the Github page, select your profile picture at the top right, then select **Settings**\n4. In the *Code, planning, and automation* section of the sidebar, select **Codespaces**.\n5. To the right of *Codespaces secrets*, select **New secret**.\n6. Under *Name*, name your secret **REWARDS_CLIENT_ID**.\n7. Under *Value*, paste the Client ID value you copied in Step 1.\n8. Select the *Repository access* drop-down menu, add **OktaEdu/okta-learning-sign-in-users-and-secure-sessions**.\n9. Select **Add Secret**.\n\n>Ignore any prompts to reload your Codespace. We will do this once we're done adding secrets.",
      "line": 18,
      "title": "Configure the Rewards application appClientId"
    },
    {
      "file": "redirect/rewards",
      "description": "## Configure the Rewards application `baseOktaURL`\n\nLet's repeat the process to add `BASE_OKTA_URL` to GitHub secrets. This will be used on the line highlighted above.\n\n1. In your **Okta Admin Console**, select your username in the top right hand corner and you should see your Okta org's URL.\n\n2. Hover your mouse over the URL and select **Copy to clipboard**.\n3. Switch back to the **GitHub** tab and select **New secret**.\n6. Under *Name*, name your secret **BASE_OKTA_ORG**.\n7. Under *Value*, enter https:// and then paste the value you copied in Step 2.\n8. Select the *Repository access* drop-down menu, add **OktaEdu/okta-learning-sign-in-users-and-secure-sessions**.\n9. Select **Add Secret**.\n\n>Ignore any prompts to reload your Codespace. We will do this once we're done adding secrets.\n",
      "line": 17,
      "title": "Configure the Rewards application baseOktaURL"
    },
    {
      "title": "Create an Application Integration for the CRM app",
      "description": "## Create an Application Integration for the CRM app\n\nWe will complete the same steps for the Franchisee app. The one difference is we will assign this app to the Franchisee group. See how many steps you can complete without referring to the instructions!\n\n1. Back in Okta, select **Back to Applications**\n\n2. Select **Create App Integration**\n\n3. Select the **OIDC - OpenID Connect** radio button.\n\n4. Select the **Single-Page Application** radio button.\n\n5. Select **Next**\n\n6. Name this application **Franchisee CRM**\n\n7.  In the **Sign-in redirect URIs** field, enter the value that prints out to the terminal when you click [here](command:codetour.sendTextToTerminal?[\"echo https://$CODESPACE_NAME-8080.app.github.dev/redirect/crm\"]).\n\n7. In the **Sign-out redirect URIs** field, enter the value that prints out to the terminal when you click [here](command:codetour.sendTextToTerminal?[\"echo https://$CODESPACE_NAME-8080.app.github.dev\"]).\n\n\n8. Under **Assignments** select the radio button option for **Limit access to select groups**\n\n9. Type in and select **Franchisees**\n\n10. Select **Save**",
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 19,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 20,
          "character": 1
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "file": "redirect/crm",
      "description": "## Configure the CRM application `appClientId`\n\n\nLet's create `CRM_CLIENT_ID`, which will be read in on the line highlighted above.\n\n1. Copy the `Client ID` that was displayed after you saved your integration. \n\n2. Return to your Github tab and select **New secret**.\n6. Under *Name*, name your secret **CRM_CLIENT_ID**.\n7. Under *Value*, paste the Client ID value you copied in Step 1.\n8. Select the *Repository access* drop-down menu, add **OktaEdu/okta-learning-sign-in-users-and-secure-sessions**.\n9. Select **Add Secret**.\n\n> When prompted by Codespaces, select **Reload to apply**. This must be done so your environment will have access to the three secrets you added. \n",
      "line": 18,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 1,
          "character": 4
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 1,
          "character": 33
        }
      },
      "title": "Configure the CRM application appClientId"
    },
    {
      "title": "Configure an Authentication Policy",
      "description": "## Configure the Authentication Policy\n\n>An Authentication Policy enforces how (with what authenticators) an end user can log in to an application. For the purpose of this lab, we'll create a **Password Only policy** to assign to our two apps so that you do not have to set up MFA for the test end-user accounts. In real-world use cases, a stronger Authentication Policy is recommended.\n\n1. Go to **Security** > **Authentication Policies**.\n2. Select the **Add a policy** button.\n3. Name the policy **Password Only** and select the **Save** button.\n4. Next to the Catch-all Rule, select the **Actions** drop down and select **Edit**.\n5. Scroll down to the THEN section. Next to **`AND` User must authenticate with**, select **Password** from the drop down.\n6. Select the **Save** button.\n\n>You may have to reauthenticate since this is a sensitive operation.\n"
    },
    {
      "title": "Assign apps the Password Only policy",
      "description": "## Assign apps the Password Only policy\n\n1. While still on the Password Only Authentication Policy page, select the **Applications** tab.\n2. Select the **Add app** button.\n3. Select the **Add button** for both **Customer Rewards** and **Franchise CRM**.\n4. Select **Done**."
    },
    {
      "title": "Start the web server",
      "description": "## Start the web server\n\n1. [Click here to toggle open the Integrated terminal](command:workbench.action.terminal.toggleTerminal) here in VSCode.\n\n2. Notice that the terminal automatically opens to the project directory.\n\n3. You can start a web server on port 8080 by issuing the following command in this terminal:\n\n ```bash\n npm run\n ```\n\nIf you don't want to type this in manually, you can alternatively [click here](command:codetour.sendTextToTerminal?[\" npm run\"]) to issue the command automatically.\n\nOnce the web server has started successfully, you will see the following message printed to the terminal: \n\n    `App running on port 8080`\n\n\n\n4.  Select **Make public** in the window that pops up at the bottom right of your environment.",
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 1,
          "character": 4
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 1,
          "character": 24
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Test the Rewards app",
      "description": "## Test out the Rewards app\n\nBefore following the directions below, that ensure you are **logged out** of any Okta sessions. **DO NOT use an Incognito browser window** for the steps that follow.\n\n1. In this environement, select the **PORTS** tab. \n\n2. Hover over the **Forwarded Address** column and select the globe icon. This will launch your web app in a new browser tab.\n\n2. Select the **Rewards App (Redirect)** link. You should be redirected to Okta to sign in.\n\n3. Log in as **soraya.esfeh@example.com**.\n\nYou should be redirected back to the Rewards app and you should see some information printed to the page that we will discuss later. For now you can see Soraya's email address at the top as well as your app's Client ID at the bottom.\n"
    },
    {
      "title": "Try to access the CRM app",
      "description": "## Try to access the CRM app\n\n1. Still logged in as Soraya, select **Return to Portal**\n\n2. Select on **CRM App (Redirect)**\n\n3. The CRM application does not load because Soraya is not authorized to access this application. The CRM app is only assigned to users in the **Franchisees** group and she is not a member of that group.\n\n4. Select the **Close Okta Session** button.\n\n5. Select **Return to Portal**"
    },
    {
      "title": "Log in as a Franchisee partner",
      "description": "## Log in as a Franchisee partner\n\nNow we're going to log in as Kay West, who is a member of the Franchisees group.\n\n1. Select on **Rewards App (Redirect)**\n\n2. Enter **kay.west@example.com** as the login and select **Next**\n\n3. Notice that this user did not have an existing session with Okta before trying to access an application that is not assigned to them. In this case, we are not even prompted for a password to authenticate and we are told that the application is not assigned to the user. The user still does not have a session.\n\n4. Select **Return to Portal**\n\n5. Click **CRM App (Redirect)** \n\n6. Log in as **kay.west@example.com**.\n\nYou should be redirected back to the CRM app and you should see see Kay's email address at the top as well as your app's Client ID at the bottom. \n",
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 1,
          "character": 4
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 1,
          "character": 34
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Close the Okta session",
      "description": "## Close the Okta session\n1. Select the **Close Okta Session** button.\n\n"
    },
    {
      "title": "Lab checkpoint",
      "description": "## ✅ Checkpoint\n\nYou now know how to set up an application integration in Okta and deploy authentication using the Redirect model.\n\n## 🎉 End of Lab\n\n**You may close and [stop this Codespace](https://docs.github.com/en/codespaces/developing-in-a-codespace/stopping-and-starting-a-codespace#stopping-a-codespace).**"
    }
  ]
}
